[{:db/id "q-344629/field-1/be8a132e5ec36f879a54ab3624495e1da1ac7ce2b0a299371685f8a5e655be69" :answer/id #uuid "b41e7542-681e-5a23-906c-d6cdd54266f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{13}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344629/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "47a24c95-d598-5016-9a5d-726fdc39f6a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344629/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "14fce101-859e-5eb3-83d8-7e00861d2288" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344629/field-1/be8a132e5ec36f879a54ab3624495e1da1ac7ce2b0a299371685f8a5e655be69" :answer-field/choices ["q-344629/field-1/be8a132e5ec36f879a54ab3624495e1da1ac7ce2b0a299371685f8a5e655be69" "q-344629/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344629" :question/id #uuid "3d93bc12-fef0-5a49-ab5f-dd63888aaf92" :question/math-academy-id "q-344629" :question/problem "Find the value of $b$ that minimizes the distance between the points $(5b+3,\\,b-1)$ and $(4,\\,2).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(b)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(b) & =((5b+3)-4)^{2}+((b-1)-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(5b-1)^{2}+(b-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(25{b}^{2}-10b+1)+({b}^{2}-6b+9) \\\\  & =26{b}^{2}-16b+10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S$, we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(b).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(b)=52b-16.\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(b)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(b)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(b) & =0 \\\\ 52b-16 & =0 \\\\ b & =\\frac{4}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(b)=52>0.$\n\nTherefore, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $b=\\frac{4}{13}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-344629/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a92cdaeb-45c3-50c5-aa7d-2d07f846ea55"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344629"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-117431/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "765788b3-2128-5267-b748-785c10ce2996" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117431/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "1c687040-3db5-5749-8b95-7c8e69ccb4e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117431/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" :answer/id #uuid "9bbe218f-c77e-57e2-8d23-799abaed29b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117431/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "ccf29e7a-c191-58d5-9043-e19b0fb3c6d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117431/selection/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8" :answer/id #uuid "0350ecfc-251a-5d07-91c4-49c251790665" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117431/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "38fac432-c74c-51c6-8551-5c2928a3ea99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-117431/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer-field/choices ["q-117431/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-117431/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-117431/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" "q-117431/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-117431/selection/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-117431" :question/id #uuid "c2bdec01-a825-51e5-9ff5-4b0a8edcd369" :question/math-academy-id "q-117431" :question/problem "If the distance between the points $(a+1,a)$ and $(1,-2)$ is minimized, then $a=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(a)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(a) & =(a+1-1)^{2}+(a-(-2))^{2} \\\\  & =(a)^{2}+(a+2)^{2} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}+{a}^{2}+4a+4 \\\\  & =2{a}^{2}+4a+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(a).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(a) & =4a+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(a)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(a)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(a) & =0 \\\\ 4a+4 & =0 \\\\ a & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(-1)=4>0.$ Therefore, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $a=-1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-117431/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a92cdaeb-45c3-50c5-aa7d-2d07f846ea55"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-117431"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-210773/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "2588b45a-a552-5644-bc59-8beed65aa339" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-210773/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "788a7d5e-93e2-5eae-8c42-9eff75f8ba27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-210773/selection/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" :answer/id #uuid "e549070d-10d6-5ca9-b37c-937452d56ff2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-210773/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer/id #uuid "ab76573b-d2a8-53c1-b626-7e7dc0be4560" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-210773/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "2d972f09-67c7-5bbb-bf51-579e1a705e6b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-210773/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3a4f3428-55aa-5bb4-b054-a35ec3a25245" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-210773/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-210773/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-210773/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-210773/selection/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" "q-210773/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" "q-210773/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-210773" :question/id #uuid "8cea7bc1-b96c-588c-a09f-814f653e29d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-210773" :question/problem "If the distance between the points $(3a-4,a+2)$ and $(-2,6)$ is minimized, then $a=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(a)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(a) & ={(3a-4-(-2))}^{2}+{(a+2-6)}^{2} \\\\  & =(3a-2)^{2}+(a-4)^{2} \\\\  & =9{a}^{2}-12a+4+{a}^{2}-8a+16 \\\\  & =10{a}^{2}-20a+20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(a).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(a) & =20a-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(a)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(a)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(a) & =0 \\\\ 20a-20 & =0 \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(1)=20>0.$ Therefore, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $b=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-210773/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a92cdaeb-45c3-50c5-aa7d-2d07f846ea55"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-210773"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-210769/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "febc7e98-789f-56ae-ad94-8d57e4368997" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-210769/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "3d64a24d-fda2-5266-a633-d1ed959be92b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-210769/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "0d072de4-84c5-5590-bb76-7d24290798a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-210769/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "32a44024-f6c2-57eb-8d1f-ee96fada5d19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-210769/selection/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" :answer/id #uuid "b06b79e0-dd81-554b-9648-8e7415cdd3c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-210769/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d36af07b-389a-52ee-a98d-145a6f16b77c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-210769/selection/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" :answer-field/choices ["q-210769/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" "q-210769/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-210769/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-210769/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-210769/selection/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-210769" :question/id #uuid "1ae60c75-2edf-5043-b2ce-f62054623107" :question/math-academy-id "q-210769" :question/problem "If the distance between the points $(b-2,2b)$ and $(1,-1)$ is minimized, then $b=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(b)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(b) & =(b-2-1)^{2}+{(2b-(-1))}^{2} \\\\  & =(b-3)^{2}+(2b+1)^{2} \\\\  & ={b}^{2}-6b+9+4{b}^{2}+4b+1 \\\\  & =5{b}^{2}-2b+10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(b).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(b) & =10b-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(b)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(b)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(b) & =0 \\\\ 10b-2 & =0 \\\\ b & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(\\frac{1}{5})=10>0.$ Therefore, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $b=\\frac{1}{5}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-210769/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a92cdaeb-45c3-50c5-aa7d-2d07f846ea55"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-210769"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-316014/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "32f25e40-33ce-5db2-a546-87db04b5cb91" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-316014/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "e972cce8-25e1-546f-8537-e9871879e422" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-316014/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-316014/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-316014" :question/id #uuid "7adad3d1-c261-5cbb-9b44-58644d239717" :question/math-academy-id "q-316014" :question/problem "Find the value of $a$ that minimizes the distance between the points $(a,\\,-2a)$ and $(4,\\,-3).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(a)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(a) & =(a-4)^{2}+((-2a)-(-3))^{2} \\\\  & =(a-4)^{2}+(-2a+3)^{2} \\\\  & =({a}^{2}-8a+16)+(4{a}^{2}-12a+9) \\\\  & =5{a}^{2}-20a+25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S$, we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(a).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(a)=10a-20.\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(a)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(a)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(a) & =0 \\\\ 10a-20 & =0 \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(a)=10>0.$\n\nTherefore, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $a=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-316014/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a92cdaeb-45c3-50c5-aa7d-2d07f846ea55"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-316014"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344637/field-1/c12c014b80a81166312ed647548e2ee5f220614d2fde7a89d2e099e041117c2e" :answer/id #uuid "2597a3ed-ff01-54c6-ba46-7270e5dc99ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{17\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344637/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "9bc19be2-9b35-5071-8a7a-c34e536c9a4e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344637/field-1/c12c014b80a81166312ed647548e2ee5f220614d2fde7a89d2e099e041117c2e" :answer-field/choices ["q-344637/field-1/c12c014b80a81166312ed647548e2ee5f220614d2fde7a89d2e099e041117c2e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344637" :question/id #uuid "ad6188c5-84a1-5ea4-91d6-e360ef7fa379" :question/math-academy-id "q-344637" :question/problem "What is the smallest possible distance between the points $(-4x,\\,2x-3)$ and $(1,\\,5),$ where $x$ is a real number? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe smallest possible distance is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(x)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={((-4x)-1)}^{2}+{((2x-3)-5)}^{2} \\\\  & =(-4x-1)^{2}+(2x-8)^{2} \\\\  & =16{x}^{2}+8x+1+4{x}^{2}-32x+64 \\\\  & =20{x}^{2}-24x+65.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(x)=40x-24.\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(x)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(x)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 40x-24 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(\\frac{3}{5})=40>0.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $x=\\frac{3}{5}.$ \n\nSince $S(x)$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=\\frac{3}{5}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S(\\frac{3}{5})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-24(\\frac{3}{5})+65} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{289}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the smallest possible distance between the points $(-4x,\\,2x-3)$ and $(1,\\,5)$ is $\\frac{17\\sqrt{5}}{5}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-344637/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a903373-151e-5744-b97c-760440f44238"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344637"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344635/field-1/f3d9aca68911cc53d57439a02bb3be1d4ce246087f542d0ed69fb92ca9322d00" :answer/id #uuid "fb00d0dd-223e-592b-8d15-1d9a729d55a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344635/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "920fae60-9add-519a-89d8-51e6f144ae42" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344635/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "c27f1987-f21f-5986-a0ec-c3724bd095a6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344635/field-1/f3d9aca68911cc53d57439a02bb3be1d4ce246087f542d0ed69fb92ca9322d00" :answer-field/choices ["q-344635/field-1/f3d9aca68911cc53d57439a02bb3be1d4ce246087f542d0ed69fb92ca9322d00" "q-344635/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344635" :question/id #uuid "43031d2f-e8dc-5b6a-bf01-0a97a6d30271" :question/math-academy-id "q-344635" :question/problem "What is the smallest possible distance between the points $(4x,\\,-x)$ and $(5,\\,-1),$ where $x$ is a real number? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe smallest possible distance is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(x)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={((4x)-5)}^{2}+{((-x)-(-1))}^{2} \\\\  & =(4x-5)^{2}+(-x+1)^{2} \\\\  & =(16{x}^{2}-40x+25)+({x}^{2}-2x+1) \\\\  & =17{x}^{2}-42x+26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(x)=34x-42.\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(x)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(x)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 34x-42 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{21}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}\\,(\\frac{21}{17})=34>0.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $x=\\frac{21}{17}.$ \n\nSince $S(x)$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=\\frac{21}{17}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S\\,(\\frac{21}{17})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S\\,(\\frac{21}{17})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17{(\\frac{21}{17})}^{2}-42(\\frac{21}{17})+26} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{441}{17}-\\frac{882}{17}+26} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{17}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the smallest possible distance between the points $(4x,\\,-x)$ and $(5,\\,-1)$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-344635/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a903373-151e-5744-b97c-760440f44238"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344635"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-316023/field-1/25e9fb53cfadbd73e90234aa65f80db621ff6adc6ee7ac86a56284fb17a526d8" :answer/id #uuid "a0793005-6fc5-585e-9280-e7e077bb728b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{6\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-316023/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b54c0399-4ce0-50aa-bf89-eeb7acec2d87" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-316023/field-1/25e9fb53cfadbd73e90234aa65f80db621ff6adc6ee7ac86a56284fb17a526d8" :answer-field/choices ["q-316023/field-1/25e9fb53cfadbd73e90234aa65f80db621ff6adc6ee7ac86a56284fb17a526d8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-316023" :question/id #uuid "16182e1a-298f-50f8-bdb7-e58926f8fcb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-316023" :question/problem "What is the smallest possible distance between the points $(b,2b)$ and $(3,0),$ where $b$ is a real number? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe smallest possible distance is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(b)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(b) & ={(b-3)}^{2}+{(2b-0)}^{2} \\\\  & =(b-3)^{2}+(2b)^{2} \\\\  & =({b}^{2}-6b+9)+(4{b}^{2}) \\\\  & =5{b}^{2}-6b+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(b).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(b)=10b-6.\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(b)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(b)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(b) & =0 \\\\ 10b-6 & =0 \\\\ b & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}\\,(\\frac{3}{5})=10>0.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $b=\\frac{3}{5}.$ \n\nSince $S(b)$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $b=\\frac{3}{5}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S\\,(\\frac{3}{5})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S\\,(\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-6(\\frac{3}{5})+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{5}-\\frac{18}{5}+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{36}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the smallest possible distance between the points $(b,2b)$ and $(3,0)$ is $\\frac{6\\sqrt{5}}{5}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-316023/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a903373-151e-5744-b97c-760440f44238"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-316023"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344638/field-1/33f7ef5cacc660e38cfdd5def7c280ebfc97a4d91f64d089adb816673b2ed09c" :answer/id #uuid "bc00d615-bc93-55ec-b924-a2c2b906f3c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{12\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344638/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "35544a26-08d5-5ba2-8b5f-9b5247115e68" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344638/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "7459ed4a-69d0-57de-8c5b-93c18d5b32bb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344638/field-1/33f7ef5cacc660e38cfdd5def7c280ebfc97a4d91f64d089adb816673b2ed09c" :answer-field/choices ["q-344638/field-1/33f7ef5cacc660e38cfdd5def7c280ebfc97a4d91f64d089adb816673b2ed09c" "q-344638/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344638" :question/id #uuid "09b5873b-e55f-5686-8fc8-75564948e205" :question/math-academy-id "q-344638" :question/problem "What is the smallest possible distance between the points $(2a-1,\\,a)$ and $(3,\\,-4),$ where $a$ is a real number? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe smallest possible distance is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(a)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(a) & ={((2a-1)-3)}^{2}+{(a-(-4))}^{2} \\\\  & =(2a-4)^{2}+(a+4)^{2} \\\\  & =4{a}^{2}-16a+16+{a}^{2}+8a+16 \\\\  & =5{a}^{2}-8a+32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(a).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(a)=10a-8.\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(a)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(a)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(a) & =0 \\\\ 10a-8 & =0 \\\\ a & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(\\frac{4}{5})=10>0.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $a=\\frac{4}{5}.$ \n\nSince $S(a)$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $a=\\frac{4}{5}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S(\\frac{4}{5})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(\\frac{4}{5})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}-8(\\frac{4}{5})+32} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{144}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the smallest possible distance between the points $(2a-1,\\,a)$ and $(3,\\,-4)$ is $\\frac{12\\sqrt{5}}{5}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-344638/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a903373-151e-5744-b97c-760440f44238"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344638"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-210779/selection/5dedd3fc97eb64beda5d906ad6dd87aaae34db54c1ed35ec3a4b810f0ed5b6e3" :answer/id #uuid "577783d3-4a1d-5530-84b2-ed0bd8042ad3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-210779/selection/219347bb4a6f51134d7f5aff5da33544f44afee934064621ebff9cf9e335ee59" :answer/id #uuid "69520b36-52ae-5fd7-a274-674a9896939d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-210779/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "2703946c-d8f7-5039-a77f-771fadc819b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-210779/selection/f79a481304c3dca8e90c1cc75397525ca1d3328745d1908a3964cfc0064c67cc" :answer/id #uuid "fb46060b-57f3-5424-b3fa-1e876aa49a54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-210779/selection/8de7a84a41666d4612460439d17949f75cc9ba4883ff75d5034a1786c2a06792" :answer/id #uuid "6d9a12d2-cf3b-5deb-83a8-c6513b5758f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-210779/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7c8d5b58-54ac-54ed-bb0b-b46c70c0b44b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-210779/selection/f79a481304c3dca8e90c1cc75397525ca1d3328745d1908a3964cfc0064c67cc" :answer-field/choices ["q-210779/selection/5dedd3fc97eb64beda5d906ad6dd87aaae34db54c1ed35ec3a4b810f0ed5b6e3" "q-210779/selection/219347bb4a6f51134d7f5aff5da33544f44afee934064621ebff9cf9e335ee59" "q-210779/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-210779/selection/f79a481304c3dca8e90c1cc75397525ca1d3328745d1908a3964cfc0064c67cc" "q-210779/selection/8de7a84a41666d4612460439d17949f75cc9ba4883ff75d5034a1786c2a06792"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-210779" :question/id #uuid "de6048fb-f4be-59a2-8d90-a6da46cf2484" :question/math-academy-id "q-210779" :question/problem "What is the smallest possible distance between the points $(a,2a-1)$ and $(4,-2),$ where $a$ is a real number?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(a)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(a) & ={(a-4)}^{2}+{(2a-1-(-2))}^{2} \\\\  & =(a-4)^{2}+(2a+1)^{2} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}-8a+16+4{a}^{2}+4a+1 \\\\  & =5{a}^{2}-4a+17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(a).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(a) & =10a-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(a)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(a)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(a) & =0 \\\\ 10a-4 & =0 \\\\ a & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(\\frac{2}{5})=10>0.$ So, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $a=\\frac{2}{5}.$ \n\nSince $S(a)$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $a=\\frac{2}{5}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S(\\frac{2}{5})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}-4(\\frac{2}{5})+17} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{81}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-210779/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a903373-151e-5744-b97c-760440f44238"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-210779"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344721/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "1fc0cdcf-f8ba-5c5d-862d-67d2e76a88f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344721/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "4c678ee4-bec1-555e-956f-4f71df8ab5d1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344721/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer-field/choices ["q-344721/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344721/field-2/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "b5335f2c-33bf-5845-8b81-eccba23c531b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344721/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "4bab923f-4219-584e-93fc-86f1c2ccf7c9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344721/field-2/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer-field/choices ["q-344721/field-2/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344721" :question/id #uuid "8a718395-8763-5f75-bc97-e930d48614c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-344721" :question/problem "Use calculus to find the coordinates of the closest point to $P(4,5)$ that is equidistant to the points $A(-7,2)$ and $B(3,2).$\n\nThe coordinates of the required point are\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/assets/e283660466c42f7723d9e044bb2eefe148c468f7dae5a1b5882e2589e7e5f60a.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(4,5)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-4)^{2}+(y-5)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+(y-5)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8x-10y+41\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(-7,2)$ and $B(3,2).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^{2}=(QB)^{2},$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(x+7)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QA)^{2}} & ={{(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $x$ the subject in the constraint equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+7)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+14x+49+(y-2)^{2} & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+(y-2)^{2} \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{(y-2)^{2}}+14x+49 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}-6x+9+\\cancel{(y-2)^{2}} \\\\ 14x+6x & =9-49 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(y) & =(-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}-8(-2)-10y+41 \\\\  & =4+{y}^{2}+16-10y+41 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}-10y+61.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(y)=2y-10,\n$$\n\nand solve ${S}^{′}(y)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 2y-10 & =0 \\\\ y & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(5)=2>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $y=5$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nTherefore, the required point is $(\\,-2,5\\,).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-344721/field-1" "q-344721/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90a673b6-4025-5075-9a8a-120b90bc4f45"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344721"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-87502/selection/228d7b42fcb34515143bb5c9b68cc81052255c360fff1053199a2147dbd7eb12" :answer/id #uuid "91e6b082-6609-50ff-bc91-a8b6158d69fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-87502/selection/265033e705ddbe9f1f30f47188b03a2b28b2a12a3ed7f75cd554e6baee2550bc" :answer/id #uuid "da861823-2efe-568f-854f-0b9c28309e28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-87502/selection/49dd955f7246aaaf8b0009bb70935ca10c0397c18703e7d61cbb9a56e3d7ae6f" :answer/id #uuid "8b8fa9e6-a301-554c-94ec-a055854bbfe1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-87502/selection/85cb4fac0396602d43dca2c00e3ca71b29816b8e6c6a13d1cfa7f3ced25f7b78" :answer/id #uuid "c427ca53-8ad6-5c9b-85dc-0dd6eb155952" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-87502/selection/9227689a53ffed4a0bb68e6ec54ed701bb29f9a8d0acfc86ca1343251d753879" :answer/id #uuid "9b49fb41-3951-5a63-8545-505abdd7c72c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-87502/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "997ef232-78a3-5863-88f1-f3a914866af1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-87502/selection/49dd955f7246aaaf8b0009bb70935ca10c0397c18703e7d61cbb9a56e3d7ae6f" :answer-field/choices ["q-87502/selection/228d7b42fcb34515143bb5c9b68cc81052255c360fff1053199a2147dbd7eb12" "q-87502/selection/265033e705ddbe9f1f30f47188b03a2b28b2a12a3ed7f75cd554e6baee2550bc" "q-87502/selection/49dd955f7246aaaf8b0009bb70935ca10c0397c18703e7d61cbb9a56e3d7ae6f" "q-87502/selection/85cb4fac0396602d43dca2c00e3ca71b29816b8e6c6a13d1cfa7f3ced25f7b78" "q-87502/selection/9227689a53ffed4a0bb68e6ec54ed701bb29f9a8d0acfc86ca1343251d753879"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-87502" :question/id #uuid "ca30ce88-7e10-5fc4-80de-119f48311a4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-87502" :question/problem "Use calculus to find the closest point to $P(1,2)$ that is equidistant to the points $A(-2,0)$ and $B(1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/assets/052b405c104f0d6bc353782c6e07bfb1e0245726d86346a545ea38aa31941711.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(1,2)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x-4y+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(-2,0)$ and $B(1,3).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^{2}=(QB)^{2},$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(x-(-2))^{2}+(y-0)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QA)^{2}} & ={{(x-1)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4x+4 & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{y}^{2}-6y+9 \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}+4x+4 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}-2x+1+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-6y+9 \\\\ 6y & =-6x+10-4 \\\\ y & =-x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+(-x+1)^{2}-2x-4(-x+1)+5 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{x}^{2}-2x+1-2x+4x-4+5 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =4x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve ${S}^{′}(x)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 4x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(0)=4>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $x=0$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nFinally, the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\ny=-0+1=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required point is $(0,1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-87502/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90a673b6-4025-5075-9a8a-120b90bc4f45"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-87502"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344720/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "16306b8f-909d-552a-8b05-38589b173a7e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344720/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "66aa18e1-391c-54a4-97aa-fa042554cd68" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344720/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-344720/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344720/field-2/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "9c21e134-4acb-5d43-bba6-bb5051ade958" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344720/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "233239f0-b40f-5440-98ef-ae89cf00e3ca" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344720/field-2/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer-field/choices ["q-344720/field-2/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344720" :question/id #uuid "ab65483d-ab43-55de-ae49-174136d85e59" :question/math-academy-id "q-344720" :question/problem "Use calculus to find the coordinates of the closest point to $P(5,-3)$ that is equidistant to the points $A(-2,1)$ and $B(6,1).$\n\nThe coordinates of the required point are\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/assets/a86fbc456930e18159b1745f5a71fe82bf7075f3aa6d9fd4307a71a583a37a7a.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(5,-3)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-5)^{2}+(y-(-3))^{2} \\\\  & =(x-5)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10x+6y+34\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(-2,1)$ and $B(6,1).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^{2}=(QB)^{2},$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(x+2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QA)^{2}} & ={{(x-6)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $x$ the subject in the constraint equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =(x-6)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+4+(y-1)^{2} & ={x}^{2}-12x+36+(y-1)^{2} \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{(y-1)^{2}}+4x+4 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}-12x+36+\\cancel{(y-1)^{2}} \\\\ 4x+12x & =36-4 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(y) & =(2)^{2}+{y}^{2}-10(2)+6y+34 \\\\  & =4+{y}^{2}-20+6y+34 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}+6y+18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(y)=2y+6,\n$$\n\nand solve ${S}^{′}(y)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 2y+6 & =0 \\\\ y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(-3)=2>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $y=-3$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nTherefore, the required point is $(\\,2,-3\\,).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-344720/field-1" "q-344720/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90a673b6-4025-5075-9a8a-120b90bc4f45"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344720"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-117463/selection/7facf29346ad9a49d2aface5f71efa67a2855b2172df3719c784027991063b25" :answer/id #uuid "ef7245a1-f836-5477-80bb-9d01eac07cf9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(6,\\frac{7}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117463/selection/a9d7432baf25855d7eb64bd973d424fe1ed0cb7897a309f33b7b2118b16d6b14" :answer/id #uuid "0a79193b-06b0-5c9b-9318-55b0e9bfac35" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,\\frac{7}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117463/selection/93fb82ac5f7f057f85da1fe86c7a963325ca548ec91a734e7ea380e050beb566" :answer/id #uuid "20e26f9b-3d57-5c0c-b2a6-31394a91b146" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(6,4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117463/selection/baf6717f0b91bd845d575bdfd24bd3db21b6c6e36f9ee7d8f87ca17b94a93e77" :answer/id #uuid "6846ed55-290f-54d6-89aa-da529928996b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(6,3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117463/selection/440b08a26efff1ff8f3a7de221104fcfff5aab0a1fd1bca92dbdd73c77bd730e" :answer/id #uuid "0d317be4-9289-54dc-8a4c-b8aefcdebc41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{7}{2},6)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117463/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f0bafbab-826b-5858-9336-bdefd1956b14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-117463/selection/7facf29346ad9a49d2aface5f71efa67a2855b2172df3719c784027991063b25" :answer-field/choices ["q-117463/selection/7facf29346ad9a49d2aface5f71efa67a2855b2172df3719c784027991063b25" "q-117463/selection/a9d7432baf25855d7eb64bd973d424fe1ed0cb7897a309f33b7b2118b16d6b14" "q-117463/selection/93fb82ac5f7f057f85da1fe86c7a963325ca548ec91a734e7ea380e050beb566" "q-117463/selection/baf6717f0b91bd845d575bdfd24bd3db21b6c6e36f9ee7d8f87ca17b94a93e77" "q-117463/selection/440b08a26efff1ff8f3a7de221104fcfff5aab0a1fd1bca92dbdd73c77bd730e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-117463" :question/id #uuid "a4090e1f-8550-5b4c-a178-5b789c571ba4" :question/math-academy-id "q-117463" :question/problem "Use calculus to find the closest point to $P(6,7)$ that is equidistant to the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(0,6).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/assets/c4fd419e12879af4910e5d8364426c14342c3dadb970d8bbc43d31213a6ff947.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(6,7)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-6)^{2}+(y-7)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12x-14y+85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(0,6).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^{2}=(QB)^{2},$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(x-0)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QA)^{2}} & ={{(x-0)^{2}+(y-6)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-0)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-6)^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y+1 & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12y+36 \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-2y+1 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-12y+36 \\\\ 10y & =35 \\\\ y & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12x-14y+85 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-12x-14(\\frac{7}{2})+85 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-12x+\\frac{193}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =2x-12,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve ${S}^{′}(x)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2x-12 & =0 \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(6)=2>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $x=6$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nFinally, the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required point is $(6,\\frac{7}{2}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-117463/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90a673b6-4025-5075-9a8a-120b90bc4f45"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-117463"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-87515/selection/05c608960f55431a8a5ad458c5c69ad50acf9102fe96e8fa5ccb2f47a809adde" :answer/id #uuid "16ee3a6c-8ccf-5f04-8697-70869c8f3270" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-87515/selection/ca6af7ae67072899bb0fa54a714cd69fb79e694a5b3fb02e9c2249473dfce856" :answer/id #uuid "be791fd6-3a7d-5295-87a5-d69f2957f478" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(4,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-87515/selection/84afada78233dbad98988ae4de1152957e160f0282b9e41020f782244fe89dc0" :answer/id #uuid "43dc5571-2daf-53ff-9b8e-2bb2848d07a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-87515/selection/984f28fd2eb6305d893eef6bf628469d4c7b55d83d8e33de0935c83142e13846" :answer/id #uuid "5d98d7f6-0535-5b96-9809-21ac7cc3a114" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-87515/selection/fc4c694d6c016efaa9e5cfb8689eb85b0ea02cd1224f188bd6d40312e0d1ed0c" :answer/id #uuid "52f3d599-ae9d-5ea2-aab1-261ade0146f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(4,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-87515/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "70b1e9b0-1a90-59ac-9a5a-9d7052933325" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-87515/selection/984f28fd2eb6305d893eef6bf628469d4c7b55d83d8e33de0935c83142e13846" :answer-field/choices ["q-87515/selection/05c608960f55431a8a5ad458c5c69ad50acf9102fe96e8fa5ccb2f47a809adde" "q-87515/selection/ca6af7ae67072899bb0fa54a714cd69fb79e694a5b3fb02e9c2249473dfce856" "q-87515/selection/84afada78233dbad98988ae4de1152957e160f0282b9e41020f782244fe89dc0" "q-87515/selection/984f28fd2eb6305d893eef6bf628469d4c7b55d83d8e33de0935c83142e13846" "q-87515/selection/fc4c694d6c016efaa9e5cfb8689eb85b0ea02cd1224f188bd6d40312e0d1ed0c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-87515" :question/id #uuid "caa23b2f-245e-57e1-8795-44493413dcd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-87515" :question/problem "Use calculus to find the closest point to $P(2,2)$ that is equidistant to the points $A(0,2)$ and $B(4,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/assets/8be39e6dde084154ecfd43718cb76352c69d26bd76d520177a6c41ee69a7f18e.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(2,2)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x-4y+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(0,2)$ and $B(4,-2).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^{2}=(QB)^{2},$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(x-0)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QA)^{2}} & ={{(x-4)^{2}+(y-(-2))^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =(x-4)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4y+4 & ={x}^{2}-8x+16+{y}^{2}+4y+4 \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-4y+4 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}-8x+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}+4y+20 \\\\ 8y & =8x-16 \\\\ y & =x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+(x-2)^{2}-4x-4\\cdot (x-2)+8 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{x}^{2}-4x+4-4x-4x+8+8 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-12x+20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =4x-12,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve ${S}^{′}(x)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 4x-12 & =0 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(3)=4>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $x=3$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nFinally, the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\ny=3-2=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required point is $(3,1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-87515/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90a673b6-4025-5075-9a8a-120b90bc4f45"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-87515"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12605"] :question/problem "If the distance between the points $(b+3,b)$ and $(-1, 2)$ is minimized, find the value of $b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(b)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(b) & =(b+3-(-1))^{2}+{(b-2)}^{2} \\\\  & =(b+4)^{2}+(b-2)^{2} \\\\  & ={b}^{2}+8b+16+{b}^{2}-4b+4 \\\\  & =2{b}^{2}+4b+20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative $S'(b).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(b) & =4b+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(b)$ are the points where the derivative $S'(b) = 0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(b) & =0 \\\\ 4b+4 & =0 \\\\ b & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that $S''\\left(-1\\right) =4> 0.$ Therefore, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $b = -1.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12594"] :question/problem "What is the smallest possible distance between the points $(a+1, a+2)$ and $(-1, 3),$ where $a$ is a real number?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(a)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(a) & =(a+1-(-1))^{2}+(a+2-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(a+2)^{2}+(a-1)^{2} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}+4a+4+{a}^{2}-2a+1 \\\\  & =2{a}^{2}+2a+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative $S'(a).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(a) & =4a+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(a)$ are the points where the derivative $S'(a) = 0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(a) & =0 \\\\ 4a+2 & =0 \\\\ a & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that $S''\\left(- \\dfrac 12\\right) =4 > 0.$ So, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $a = -\\dfrac12.$ \n\nSince $S(a)$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $a=-\\dfrac 12$ is given by\n\n$$\nd = \\sqrt{S\\left(-\\dfrac 12\\right)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+2(-\\frac{1}{2})+5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12595"] :question/problem "Use calculus to find the closest point to $P(3,-2)$ that is equidistant to the points $A(-1,1)$ and $B(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/assets/a22e7e19b360c91bcdae8eba8a3230943bd69270eac9dd158652d1648e24de51.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overline{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(3,-2)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-(-2))^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+{y}^{2}+4y+4 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6x+4y+13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(-1,1)$ and $B(2,0).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^2 = (QB)^2,$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(x-(-1))^{2}+(y-1)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QA)^{2}} & ={{(x-2)^{2}+(y-0)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1+{y}^{2}-2y+1 & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{y}^{2} \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+2x+1+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-2y+1 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}-4x+4+\\cancel{{y}^{2}} \\\\ 2x-2y+2 & =-4x+4 \\\\ 6x-2 & =2y \\\\ y & =3x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+(3x-1)^{2}-6x+4(3x-1)+13 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+9{x}^{2}-6x+1-6x+12x-4+13 \\\\  & =10{x}^{2}+10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =20x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve $S'(x)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 20x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\nS''(0) =20>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $x=0$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nFinally, the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\ny = 3(0)-1 = -1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required point is $\\left(0,-1\\right).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
